# Requests against `uv run fraud serve` (127.0.0.1:8000), in the order you would try them.
# Customer c00042 exists in the generated history: NL card, spends around EUR 30 on a known phone.

### Health: which model version is serving
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/health

### An ordinary purchase: known device, home country, afternoon. Expect approve.
POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/score
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transaction_id": "demo-1",
  "timestamp": "2026-05-02T14:00:00+02:00",
  "customer_id": "c00042",
  "customer_country": "NL",
  "merchant_id": "m00010",
  "merchant_country": "NL",
  "amount": 35.5,
  "device_id": "c00042-d0"
}

### Three in the morning, abroad, unknown device, EUR 1,890. Expect decline with reasons.
POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/score
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transaction_id": "demo-2",
  "timestamp": "2026-05-02T03:12:00+02:00",
  "customer_id": "c00042",
  "customer_country": "NL",
  "merchant_id": "m01234",
  "merchant_country": "US",
  "amount": 1890.0,
  "device_id": "unknown-phone"
}

### The same transaction id again: the identical response, and it is not counted twice.
POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/score
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transaction_id": "demo-2",
  "timestamp": "2026-05-02T03:12:00+02:00",
  "customer_id": "c00042",
  "customer_country": "NL",
  "merchant_id": "m01234",
  "merchant_country": "US",
  "amount": 1890.0,
  "device_id": "unknown-phone"
}

